    "lore": "Having led the mainstream Tradicionalistas into an unhappy compromise with Franco, José María Valiente Soriano faced a Falangist purge of his communion. The final indignity. A man of caution, his policy that the one true king should remain outside Spain until the rising's end has prevailed. Until then, Valiente serves as Don Javier's leal regent and President of the Council of Ministers.\n\nThe regent is old: old enough to appreciate this long-missed unity. With the full backing of the self-styled Javier I thrown behind Valiente's Requeté militias, the estranged Carlist family has returned to the reconstituted Comunión Tradicionalista. Unable to control the circumstances which caused the initial splits, experience inherited and perfected by time will serve him well in leading the resurgent movement.\n\nFor the first time in a hundred years, the movement has the numbers to independently fight for God. Carlism's prospects are full to bursting. Decentralized in belief and organization, and priding itself on its rejection of the parliamentary-party model, the CT will prove an unruly apparatus for controlling its congregation. However, currently only divided over the king's presence, the communion's sole fixation is to pursue this last, best chance for restoration. Under heaven, tradition will reign again.\n\nThe Cross of Burgundy marches again; a weathered red beret is donned one, last time."
